  "image_name": "4e2df20473fff40e5ac0f6527be8fe25.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure outlines a self-amplifying autoimmune process that drives biliary damage. It begins when a microbial mimic and altered mitochondrial PDC-E2 appear to activate antigen-presenting cells and T cells. An APC presents antigen to a CD4+ T cell through MHC II and costimulation, which then helps activate B cells and CD8+ T cells. B cells differentiate into plasma cells that produce antimitochondrial antibodies, shown binding mitochondrial material released in apoptotic blebs from injured biliary epithelial cells.\n\nA key idea is that these epithelial cells seem unusually vulnerable because apoptotic cells retain or expose modified PDC-E2, making them targets for immune recognition. Antibody-bound antigen and cell debris are then taken up again by APCs, reinforcing CD4+ T-cell activation. On the right, helper subsets, especially Tfh and Tfr cells, regulate antibody production, while CD8+ T cells, tissue-resident T cells, and NK cells in the liver contribute to further injury.\n\nOverall, the diagram depicts a feed-forward loop linking innate sensing, autoreactive T-cell help, B-cell antibody production, and direct cytotoxic attack, culminating in ongoing biliary injury and persistence of the autoimmune response.",
